<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/16th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1559">1559</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-war-of-1551-1559">Italian War of 1551–1559</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis consisted of treaties signed in April 1559 between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and between France and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, ending more than sixty years of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. France renounced its Italian claims but kept Calais and the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metz">Metz</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toul">Toul</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emmanuel-philibert-duke-of-savoy">Emmanuel Philibert of Savoy</a> recovered most of his duchy. The settlement left Spain dominant in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> for over a century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/peace-of-cateau-cambresis.jpg" alt="Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cateau-Cambresis.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After 1535 it became a province of the Habsburg monarchy, confirmed to Spain by the <b>Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> in 1559, and its revenues and soldiers served wars fought elsewhere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Emmanuel Philibert, serving Philip II of Spain, commanded the Spanish army that crushed the French at the Battle of Saint-Quentin in 1557, and the <b>Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> in 1559 restored most of his lands.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II’s forces defeated France at Saint-Quentin in 1557 and Gravelines in 1558, leading to the <b>Treaty of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> in 1559.</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Peace of Cateau-Cambrésis</b> between France and Spain in 1559 withdrew French support, and Montalcino surrendered to Cosimo I de' Medici, joining the future Grand Duchy of Tuscany.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-siena-at-montalcino">Republic of Siena at Montalcino</a></li></ul></section>
